53	13093237-0938-452c-a5c3-63affacbe9d0	\N	52	54	catalog-pg-amproc.html	html	\N	\N	52.5.pg_amproc#	\N	\N	\N	\N	52.5.pg_amproc#	\N	\N	\N	\N	18	en	{"title": "52.5.pg_amproc#", "filename": "catalog-pg-amproc.html", "chunk_count": 1, "chunk_index": 0}	Page: 52.5.pg_amproc#\nSource: catalog-pg-amproc.html\n\n52.5.\npg_amproc\n#\nThe catalog\npg_amproc\nstores information about support functions associated with access method operator families. There is one row for each support function belonging to an operator family.\nTable 52.5.\npg_amproc\nColumns\nColumn Type\nDescription\noid\noid\nRow identifier\namprocfamily\noid\n(references\npg_opfamily\n.\noid\n)\nThe operator family this entry is for\namproclefttype\noid\n(references\npg_type\n.\noid\n)\nLeft-hand input data type of associated operator\namprocrighttype\noid\n(references\npg_type\n.\noid\n)\nRight-hand input data type of associated operator\namprocnum\nint2\nSupport function number\namproc\nregproc\n(references\npg_proc\n.\noid\n)\nOID of the function\nThe usual interpretation of the\namproclefttype\nand\namprocrighttype\nfields is that they identify the left and right input types of the operator(s) that a particular support function supports. For some access methods these match the input data type(s) of the support function itself, for others not. There is a notion of\n“\ndefault\n”\nsupport functions for an index, which are those with\namproclefttype\nand\namprocrighttype\nboth equal to the index operator class's\nopcintype\n.	cbf91015186946d162364429c04dfc3bdd974d062ce61835c1621ec694a9c049	\N	0	1	bge-base-en-v1.5	[0.12654144,1.3287406,-2.7883468,-1.9503831,1.0162795,-1.2545644,0.6090723,-0.10281542,0.079240166,0.00014079915,-0.4869638,0.8387801,1.0164989,-0.20620435,0.711069,-1.0201168,-0.2742066,-0.29095384,-0.24422053,-0.48832023,0.17485082,-0.44036385,-0.8846345,0.6068552,1.334916,1.1066785,0.07711847,-0.24861684,-0.886374,0.24041876,0.423529,-0.32652357,0.059755985,-0.62572426,-0.49409288,-0.89637935,0.73