722	fbff222a-9c7b-44eb-9286-e5f46180d58d	\N	721	723	queries-order.html	html	\N	\N	7.5. Sorting Rows (ORDER BY)#	\N	\N	\N	\N	7.5. Sorting Rows (ORDER BY)#	\N	\N	\N	\N	18	en	{"title": "7.5. Sorting Rows (ORDER BY)#", "filename": "queries-order.html", "chunk_count": 2, "chunk_index": 1}	Page: 7.5. Sorting Rows (ORDER BY)#\nSource: queries-order.html\n\nitem is a simple name that could match either an output column name or a column from the table expression. The output column is used in such cases. This would only cause confusion if you use\nAS\nto rename an output column to match some other table column's name.\nORDER BY\ncan be applied to the result of a\nUNION\n,\nINTERSECT\n, or\nEXCEPT\ncombination, but in this case it is only permitted to sort by output column names or numbers, not by expressions.\n[6]\nActually,\nPostgreSQL\nuses the\ndefault B-tree operator class\nfor the expression's data type to determine the sort ordering for\nASC\nand\nDESC\n. Conventionally, data types will be set up so that the\n<\nand\n>\noperators correspond to this sort ordering, but a user-defined data type's designer could choose to do something different.	3ea2fcf35f0fff8e4ae5866b3e928109e7a0ba17acd029ec9f19c7f1298a199e	\N	1	2	bge-base-en-v1.5	[-0.67849433,0.79544497,-2.447107,-0.5746795,0.9163644,-1.3204879,0.9406906,-0.47950247,-0.22110967,-0.60940856,-0.46963423,0.6001664,2.49047,-0.54502934,-0.45760804,-0.41629735,-0.32130054,-0.8306464,0.2995559,0.50927794,-0.67961246,0.23821805,-0.20070131,-0.7202606,0.44343853,0.5926816,-1.1615659,0.002848925,-1.4436567,0.64753956,0.15705454,0.86426616,0.9001087,-0.08475615,-1.7770844,-0.5763848,0.8022236,0.1784725,-0.17849474,0.8350518,1.1047051,1.0451509,0.4947455,-1.1320004,-0.08542476,-0.89204884,0.8075999,0.45099768,1.354229,-1.8561336,0.61856854,-0.1789104,0.7838806,-1.0105647,0.95323545,1.5321319,0.040856056,1.0698316,1.0078135,0.7343527,0.72947866,0.6977873,-0.118563384,1.0750735,0.32398614,-0.74038655,-1.0013481,0.91139585,0.055338163,