32	how to set hugepages	To configure and enable huge pages for PostgreSQL in PostgreSQL 18:\n\n```bash\n# Check required kernel settings\ngrep -E 'CONFIG_HUGETLBFS=y|CONFIG_HUGETLB_PAGE=y' /boot/config-$(uname -r)\n\n# Query the number of huge pages needed\npostgres -D $PGDATA -C shared_memory_size_in_huge_pages\n\n# Set the number of huge pages (e.g., 3170)\nsysctl -w vm.nr_hugepages=3170\n\n# Persist settings across reboots by adding to /etc/sysctl.conf\necho "vm.nr_hugepages = 3170" >> /etc/sysctl.conf\n\n# Optionally, set non-default huge page size (e.g., 2MB)\necho 3170 > /sys/kernel/mm/hugepages/hugepages-2048kB/nr_hugepages\n```\n\nReplace `3170` with the appropriate number of huge pages based on your system requirements. Ensure you have sufficient memory to allocate for huge pages. Adjust paths and values as needed for your specific setup.	[0.0604084,2.8894186,-3.3952649,-0.9789711,-0.4565668,-0.095058426,-0.26072368,1.3343835,-0.68321335,-0.50977355,0.29190013,-0.5427934,1.9156615,0.9124224,0.036651485,0.022506064,-0.66580975,-0.9870493,-0.62419415,-0.6400238,-0.42510182,-0.94264394,-0.5323327,-0.48449203,0.922645,-0.06295632,-0.18561645,0.055768907,0.24460942,1.1494725,-0.90307444,-0.36282596,-1.7108736,0.6221325,1.0168725,0.082087085,-0.29733494,-0.16033678,-0.62236375,0.9546757,0.6887227,-1.2691526,0.53487664,-0.712609,-0.7133343,-1.1846948,0.9626758,0.50628036,1.474488,-0.96131134,0.35305136,-0.10180826,0.24815421,-1.6506749,0.48647892,0.77082145,-0.79686606,0.5910868,1.5511098,0.29461536,1.4334228,-0.32903528,0.1892634,1.9412766,-0.07958874,-0.29809636,-1.3029436,-0.37500614,0.18324095,-0.5396095,0.703385,0.6984989,0.15680596,-0.8311398,-0.32390293,-0.2919555,-0.37715098,0.2794365,-0.13396803,2.230004,-0.27654022,0.35815987,1.9741515,0.026809884,0.56359637,0.08024672,0.09684579,-0.06498566,-0.82362163,0.4060977,0.94104624,-0.3958311,0.18963966,1.3670222,0.67537165,1.349258,0.027223438,1.7105483,-1.7327191,-0.17399691,-0.17781581,1.090876,0.22547594,0.7