48	and restore_command ?	To configure and use the `restore_command`, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Set Up the Restore Command**: Define a command that will retrieve archived WAL segments from an archive directory.\n\n2. **Restart PostgreSQL**: Ensure PostgreSQL restarts for changes to take effect.\n\nHere is how you can do it:\n\n### Step 1: Configure `restore_command`\n\nEdit your `postgresql.conf` file and set the `restore_command`. For example, to use a simple shell command that retrieves files from an external directory:\n\n```markdown\nrestore_command = 'cp /mnt/archives/%f %p'\n```\n\nThis command copies archived WAL segments from `/mnt/archives/`.\n\n### Step 2: Restart PostgreSQL\n\nRestart PostgreSQL to apply the changes:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl restart postgresql\n```\n\nor if you are using a different init system:\n\n```bash\npg_ctl restart -D /path/to/data/directory\n```\n\nThis setup will enable PostgreSQL to retrieve WAL segments from the specified archive directory during recovery.	[0.6394596,-0.9786817,-4.384349,-0.9239859,1.9691277,-1.2900283,-0.27707365,0.899083,0.21834683,-1.0767739,-1.0045778,1.4556459,0.9947189,1.1016794,0.034970585,0.72344255,0.50547606,-0.47594467,0.24198715,0.22133192,1.0916164,-1.569643,0.1727431,0.042024177,2.4039931,0.96249855,0.9504003,1.0293245,-0.691851,-0.19346441,2.2425492,0.46407947,-0.18241818,-0.41492027,0.052127477,-1.181868,0.8592151,-0.0947379,-0.260046,0.5705667,0.6624408,-0.12003044,0.7938814,-0.7016808,1.0342482,-0.28836337,-0.17584226,-0.035622664,1.2668508,-1.7383193,1.1528445,-0.800326,-0.96359456,-1.3283117,1.1757925,0.4565117,0.70541865,-0.21891734,0.029697549,-0.9989092,0.6027765,0.4047551,0.4303031,1.4161505,-0.570478,0.2934654,-0.85043794,1.5350119,0.11052851,-0.88880956,0.6565002,-0.06574147,0.822557,-0.3676025,-0.59558487,-0.42064667,-1.0056946,-0.91228026,0.13486822,0.02111018,0.77127343,-0.047264982,2.659818,0.09832871,0.18685074,0.59237665,0.3055435,0.40401992,-0.13091743,0.9880083,0.31746128,-0