49	how set up pitr ?	To set up Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Enable Archival**: Ensure WAL archiving is enabled.\n2. **Configure `archive_command`**: Define a command to copy archived WAL segments.\n3. **Take an Initial Base Backup**.\n4. **Set Up Recovery Configuration**.\n\nHere are the exact commands and configurations:\n\n### Step 1: Enable Archival\n\nEdit your `postgresql.conf` file and set the following parameters:\n\n```markdown\narchive_mode = on\narchive_command = 'cp %p /path/to/archive/%f'\n```\n\nRestart PostgreSQL to apply these changes:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl restart postgresql\n# or for different init systems:\npg_ctl restart -D /path/to/data/directory\n```\n\n### Step 2: Take an Initial Base Backup\n\nTake a base backup of your database. You can use `pg_basebackup`:\n\n```bash\npg_basebackup -D /path/to/base/backup -Ft -X stream --Pg-data-dir=/path/to/pgdata\n```\n\nThis command will create a base backup that can be used for recovery.\n\n### Step 3: Set Up Recovery Configuration\n\nCreate a `recovery.conf` file in your data directory if it does not already exist:\n\n```bash\necho 'restore_command = "cp /mnt/archives/%f %p"' > /path/to/pgdata/recovery.conf\n```\n\nYou can also add other recovery settings as needed.\n\n### Step 4: Start the Server\n\nStart the PostgreSQL server to begin recovery mode. If you need to start from a specific point in time, specify it using `recovery_target_time` or another relevant parameter:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl restart postgresql\n# or for different init systems:\npg_ctl start -D /path/to/data/directory\n```\n\n### Step 5: Verify the Recovery\n\nInspect the database to ensure you have recovered to the desired state. If not, you may need to repeat steps from the beginning.\n\nBy following these steps, you can set up PITR and recover your database to a specific point in time using archived WAL segments.	[-0.32892647,0.21508564,-4.2928495,0.00031489134,1.5058538,-0.90830207,-0.0332024